    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "That feeling of the connection \"playing games\" is actually a very common frustration during migrations, but it's rarely because the network is actively trying to trick you. Instead, it's often a combination of **asynchronous propagation**, **multiple DNS providers**, and sometimes even **client-side caching** that creates that erratic, jumping sensation.\n\nHere are a few technical reasons why it might feel so active and unpredictable:\n\n1.  **Multiple DNS Providers:** If your users (or your own monitoring tools) are hitting different DNS servers (like Google DNS, Cloudflare, or your ISP's resolver), they might be querying different parts of the internet at different speeds. One server might have the new IP while another still has the old one, creating a \"ping-pong\" effect where the resolution jumps back and forth depending on which server the query hits first.\n2.  **Client-Side Caching:** Sometimes, the \"shifty\" behavior isn't just about the DNS servers; it's about the devices themselves. If a user's browser or a local router has cached the old IP address, they might get the new one from a fresh query, then immediately get the old one again when they refresh or switch networks.\n3.",
